# Track E1 KNOWN GAP (FILED, MEMORY SAFETY, HIGH) — Guard[String].get()
# double-frees when the inner is heap-carrying.
#
# CATEGORY: REGISTERED method (unlike the fabrication class E1 closes, this is
# NOT check-level fabrication — `.get()` is legitimately registered on Guard,
# so E1's terminal-fallthrough reject does NOT trip here). The bug is the
# ownership contract of the runtime `.get()`: it returns a `void*` treated as
# an OWNED T by the compiler, so the local receives a SHALLOW COPY of the Str
# struct. Both the local AND the Mutex-owned String free the same buffer at
# scope exit → double free (ASan exit 134).
#
# FIX DIRECTION (per /tmp/scoutE_report_final.md follow-up #1):
# `.get()` should return `Ref[T]` (a place-carrying borrow) for heap-carrying
# inners, not by-value. This is a distinct concern from the fabrication class
# E1 closes; kept separate so E1's REJECT arm does not accidentally regress it.
#
# HEAP-FORCED VALUE (never a literal — a literal would be a false negative per
# the CLAUDE.md durable-known_gaps rule).
#
# INTENDED (after fix): prints "hello world" once, exits cleanly under ASan.
# E1: still fails with ASan double-free; UNCHANGED by this Track.
String heaped():
    return "hello " + "world" + "-forced"

void main():
    Mutex[String] m = Mutex[String](heaped())
    Guard[String] g = m.lock()
    print(g.get())
